# You have $100 to split with a stranger. How much would you offer?
subtitle: "You have $100 to split with a stranger who can refuse. Make your offer, then hear what people really do and why low offers get turned down."
voices: {"a":"gemini:Vindemiatrix | a gentle, curious quiz host; warm","b":"gemini:Achird | a friendly co-host thinking it through; natural, never exaggerated","take":"lines"}

## You have $100 to split with a stranger. How much would you offer?
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: guth
      title: An experimental analysis of ultimatum bargaining
      authors: W. Güth, R. Schmittberger, B. Schwarze
      year: 1982
      doi: 10.1016/0167-2681(82)90011-7
      finding: 'the first ultimatum game experiment: many proposers offered close to half, and low offers were rejected'
    - id: camerer
      title: Behavioral Game Theory
      authors: C. F. Camerer
      year: 2003
      finding: across many studies, mean offers are about 40 % of the pie; offers below 20 % are rejected about half the time
    - id: engel
      title: 'Dictator games: a meta study'
      authors: C. Engel
      year: 2011
      doi: 10.1007/s10683-011-9283-7
      finding: when the receiver cannot refuse, dictators give on average about 28 %, and about two in three give something

    person you "" #2f6b8f x 180 y 555 width 160 height 180 hidden true
    text you_l "You" #2f6b8f x 180 y 425 font_size 44 hidden true
    person stranger "" #b5482e x 820 y 555 width 160 height 180 hidden true
    text stranger_l "Stranger" #b5482e x 820 y 425 font_size 44 hidden true
    box cash "$100" x 500 y 540 width 240 font_size 64 hidden true
    scale offer cites ["camerer"] x 120 y 290 width 760 min 0 max 100 value 40 ticks 5 hidden true
    path refuse #b5482e points [[120,292],[272,292]] style.stroke_width 14 hidden true
    text refuse_l "often refused" #b5482e cites ["camerer"] x 236 y 345 font_size 36 hidden true
    path fair #2f6b8f points [[424,292],[500,292]] style.stroke_width 14 hidden true
    path dict_tick #2f6b8f points [[332.8,240],[332.8,330]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text dict_l "can't refuse: ~28" #2f6b8f cites ["engel"] x 322.8 y 205 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "How much would you offer?"

A: Say I hand you a hundred dollars, but you must offer part of it to a stranger. How much would you give?
    draw cash you you_l stranger stranger_l

A: If they accept, you each keep your share. If they refuse, you both get nothing.
    draw offer

    ask "How many of the 100 dollars would you offer the stranger? Click on the line." on offer judge false store o default "40"
        right: Noted. In experiments, the typical offer is about forty.

B: Wait. Why not offer one dollar? One dollar beats nothing.
    point at.ref stranger

A: That's what the textbook says: a purely selfish stranger takes the dollar.
    point at.ref cash

A: But real strangers don't. Offers under twenty dollars are turned down about half the time.
    draw refuse refuse_l

B: They'd rather walk away with nothing than take an insult?
    point at.ref refuse_l

A: Many would.
    point at.ref refuse

A: Werner Güth and his colleagues first showed it in Germany in 1982, and it has been repeated around the world.
    text guth "Güth, 1982" #8f887c cites ["guth"] x 500 y 425 font_size 36

A: So offers cluster around forty to fifty: people offer what they expect to be accepted.
    draw fair

B: So my fairness is really fear of being turned down?
    point at.ref you

A: Partly. When the stranger cannot refuse at all, offers drop to about thirty, but most people still give something.
    draw dict_tick dict_l
---
